Acquisition attempt raw/_acquiring_20260909T012046Z invalidated (renamed to
raw/_acquiring_20260909T012046Z.invalid) at 20260909T013432Z.

Reason: this was a deliberate ACQUIRE_SMOKE_YEARS=1 smoke test of the
acquisition mechanics (started while developing pipeline/acquire.py), not a
production run. It was launched via shell backgrounding (nohup ... &) and a
Monitor-tool wait; the background process did not survive, so the run never
progressed past the availability-probe stage: availability/ holds 137 files
(68 probes x 2 files each, essentially complete, last written ~01:32Z) but
fingerprint/, variables/, partner_totals/, world_totals/, hs2/, and
eu_members/ are all empty. No fingerprint-before reading was ever taken, so
there is no risk of a stale fingerprint being reused.

Decision: invalidate and start clean rather than resume, because (a) this
was a 1-year smoke test, not the real START_YEAR..END_YEAR configured run,
so its availability probes cover the right years but its (absent) archive
data would not have been usable anyway, and (b) resuming would require
parsing 137 scattered request/response files back into the exact in-memory
probe_rows structure the running script expects, which is more engineering
risk than simply re-running the (relatively cheap, ~10-15 minute)
availability-probe stage fresh as part of a real, fully foreground,
checkpointed acquisition run.

Operational change starting now: acquisition is restructured into
resumable stages (probe, fingerprint_before, archive year-by-year,
fingerprint_after, finalize) driven by a state.json checkpoint file inside
the working _acquiring_<ts> directory, invoked in the foreground with long
Bash timeouts, chained across multiple tool calls. No shell backgrounding
and no Monitor-tool waits are used for the rest of this task.
